---
/**
 * The method.
 *
 * Answers one question: why should you believe any of this?
 *
 * This is the payload of the whole site. The thesis is not "Gödel was right".
 * It is that a search which has found nothing, and which says so, and which
 * ships the script that would catch it lying, is worth more than a search that
 * announces a discovery.
 *
 * The eight disproved claims are parsed out of README.md and the negative
 * controls out of verify.sh. Neither list is retyped here, so neither can
 * quietly shrink.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';
import Value from '../components/Value.astro';
import Scoreboard from '../components/Scoreboard.astro';
import { fact, vantage, vantageLong, num } from '../lib/facts';
import { orderedListUnder, negativeControls, inline, blobUrl } from '../lib/content';

const claims = orderedListUnder('README.md', 'Claims made here and later disproved');
const controls = negativeControls();

// The headings render counts from facts.json; the lists are parsed out of
// README.md and verify.sh. Nothing tied them together, so this page would
// happily print "8 claims" over nine items -- which is precisely what the
// margin note beside it promises will not happen.
for (const [id, parsed, where] of [
  ['scoreboard.disproved', claims.length, 'README.md'],
  ['verify.negative_controls', controls.length, 'verify.sh'],
] as const) {
  if (num(id) !== parsed) {
    throw new Error(
      `${id} is ${num(id)} but ${where} yields ${parsed}. One of them is stale.`,
    );
  }
}
const vacuous = fact('incident.vacuous_ablation');
const i219 = fact('incident.219');
const timeout = fact('incident.timeout');
const cascade = fact('cascade.status');
---

<Base
  title="The method"
  description="Eight claims this project made and later disproved, six checks built to fail on purpose, and one command that re-runs everything. A search that has found nothing and says so."
  section="method"
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="wide lede">
      <p class="kicker smallcaps">Why believe any of this</p>
      <h1>The most persuasive thing here is the list of times we were wrong.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A repository that records only its wins is not evidence of anything. This
        page is the part that makes the rest checkable, and it is deliberately the
        least flattering page on the site.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wide">
      <Scoreboard />
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="disproved">
      <h2 id="disproved">
        <Value id="scoreboard.disproved" bare /> claims made here and later disproved
      </h2>
      <p>
        Every one of these was believed, written down, and then refuted — in most
        cases by an experiment we had already run, or by a reviewer reading more
        carefully than the author. They are kept in <code>README.md</code> and this
        page reads them from there, so the list cannot quietly get shorter.
      </p>

      <ol class="claims">
        {claims.map((c) => <li set:html={inline(c)} />)}
      </ol>

      <p class="also">
        One shipped experiment was also <strong>vacuous</strong> for a period: the
        ablation {vacuous.value}. Adversarial review caught it, not the harness, which
        is the wrong way round. Every ablation now carries a mandatory consistency
        probe and the harness fails if one is missing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="Source">
      <p>
        Parsed from <a href={blobUrl('README.md')}>README.md</a> at build time. If a
        ninth claim is disproved tomorrow, this page grows a ninth entry with no edit
        to the site.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <section aria-labelledby="harness">
      <h2 id="harness">One command re-runs all of it</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on this site is checked by a single script. It takes a few minutes,
        it needs no credentials, and it will tell you if we are lying.
      </p>

      <pre class="cmd"><code>git clone https://github.com/incrediblecrab/godel-loopholes
cd godel-loopholes
./verify.sh</code></pre>

      <p>
        It runs <Value id="verify.checks" bare /> checks. It re-derives the
        Article&nbsp;V thresholds in Z3 by an independent route, re-compiles the
        Isabelle model and its ablations, checksums every source scan against the
        copy it was read from, and asserts that every load-bearing value in
        <code>data/facts.json</code> still appears in the file that owns it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also checks its own advertising. The check count quoted in
        <code>README.md</code> is compared against the number of checks that actually
        ran, so this page cannot claim a number the harness does not produce.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="The harness" facts={['verify.checks', 'verify.negative_controls']} />

    <section aria-labelledby="controls">
      <h2 id="controls">
        <Value id="verify.negative_controls" bare /> of those checks are built to fail
      </h2>
      <p>
        A test suite that has never been observed to fail is not evidence that anything
        passed. So some checks feed the tools something false and fail if the tool
        accepts it. These are read out of <code>verify.sh</code>&#8217;s own section
        headers rather than counted by hand.
      </p>

      <ul class="controls">
        {
          controls.map((c) => (
            <li>
              <span class="ctl-id tabular">§{c.id}</span>
              <span class="ctl-title">{c.title}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <p>
        The last is the one that matters most for this site: a deliberately corrupted
        <code>facts.json</code> is fed to the checker, and the harness fails if the
        checker accepts it. Without that, every fact on every page would be resting on
        a validator nobody had ever seen reject anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-219">
      <h2 id="the-219">The incident that taught us the most</h2>
      <p>
        While confirming the thresholds, Z3 printed {i219.value}.
      </p>
      <p>
        The solver was right and the label was wrong. The encoding minimised
        <code>yes</code> rather than <code>present</code>, so the larger number was an
        arbitrary witness among the models achieving the same count of yes votes.
        Nothing was unsound at any point. The formula answered exactly what had been
        written, and the English sentence attached to it claimed something the formula
        did not say.
      </p>
      <p class="lesson">
        A verified result is only as good as the correspondence between the formula and
        the sentence in English beside it, and that correspondence is the one link in
        the chain no prover checks.
      </p>
      <p>
        A second incident is worth the same attention. A timeout was reported as though
        it were a refutation, giving {timeout.value} where the harness had been written
        to expect two. Machine output has three states, and treating the third as the
        second is how a tool that cannot lie gets used to tell one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="On the record" facts={['incident.219', 'incident.timeout', 'incident.vacuous_ablation']} />

    <section aria-labelledby="vantage">
      <h2 id="vantage">Everything is fixed to one date</h2>
      <p>
        Every number on this site is the number as it stood on <strong><time datetime={vantage}>{vantageLong}</time></strong>,
        the day Gödel took his citizenship examination in Trenton. That is not
        decoration. A reader who does not see the vantage will read every figure as a
        claim about today, and most of them are not.
      </p>
      <p>
        So <em>Powell v. McCormack</em> (1969) is out of bounds, and so is the
        Twenty-fifth Amendment (1967), and so is any state count after 1912. Authority
        decided after the vantage cannot be used to settle a question asked at it, even
        when we know how it came out.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="open" class="caveat">
      <h2 id="open">What is still open, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        There is one live research question and it is not settled. Three rows of the
        silence inventory, chained together, suggest the denominator of Article&nbsp;V
        is set by the body the fraction is meant to constrain. The numbers are
        computed and committed.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are labelled <strong>{cascade.value}</strong>, because the chain rests on
        one premise we have not verified: that the quorum base is members chosen and
        sworn rather than the statutory size of the chamber. If it is the statutory
        size, the chain collapses at step one and this becomes a null result — which
        our own standard requires be written up in the same detail as a win.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until that is settled, it is not a finding, and it is not presented as one
        anywhere on this site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="The open question" facts={['cascade.status', 'silence.single_chamber']} />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .claims {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    padding-left: 1.4em;
  }

  /* The retractions are set in the disproved colour and are not shrunk,
     tucked into a disclosure, or apologised for. They are the argument. */
  .claims li {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .claims li::marker {
    color: var(--disproved);
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
  }

  .also {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--disproved);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .cmd {
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    background: var(--paper-sunk);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .controls {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .controls li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3.2rem 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    padding: 0.3em 0;
    border-top: 1px dotted var(--rule);
  }

  .controls li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--rule);
  }

  .ctl-id {
    font-family: var(--font-machine);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
  }

  .lesson {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-document);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.35;
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--graphic-strong);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .caveat {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    padding: var(--space-md) 0 var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
